CertHub.register({"id":"fortinet-fortigate","vendor":"Fortinet","name":"Fortinet NSE 4 - FortiOS 7.6 Administrator","short":"FortiGate Admin","exam":"NSE4_FGT_AD-7.6","blurb":"Core Fortinet firewall exam (part of Fortinet Certified Professional – Network Security) for network and security admins who deploy, configure and troubleshoot FortiGate on FortiOS 7.6.","status":"check","statusNote":"Fortinet publishes no domain weights; weights are estimated from the number of objectives per topic area. Objectives, 90 minutes, 50 multiple-choice questions and FortiOS 7.6.0 were read in Fortinet's official FCP - FortiGate 7.6 Administrator (FCP_FGT_AD-7.6) exam description (dated June 2025) on Sept 24, 2026. Fortinet's training pages could not be reached during this check. Search results report that FCP_FGT_AD-7.6 retired on Dec 31, 2025 and was replaced by NSE 4 - FortiOS 7.6 Administrator (NSE4_FGT_AD-7.6) with the same five areas plus logging and cloud deployment tasks, and some sources give 50–55 questions. Confirm the current exam code, question count and whether remote access VPN is still tested on the FortiOS Administrator exam page before booking.","lastVerified":"2026-09-24","notices":[],"examInfo":{"questions":"About 50 multiple-choice questions (some sources say 50–55)","minutes":90,"pass":"Pass/fail; the passing score is not published","extra":"Delivered by Pearson VUE in English and Japanese. Product version FortiOS 7.6.0."},"examSim":{"questions":50,"minutes":90},"sources":[{"label":"Fortinet Training Institute: FortiOS Administrator exam","url":"https://training.fortinet.com/local/staticpage/view.php?page=fortios_administrator_exam"},{"label":"Fortinet Certified Professional – Network Security","url":"https://training.fortinet.com/local/staticpage/view.php?page=fcp_network_security"},{"label":"FCP - FortiGate 7.6 Administrator exam description (PDF)","url":"https://nseti-pdfs.s3.us-west-2.amazonaws.com/desc/FortiGate_7.6_Administrator_Exam_Description.pdf"},{"label":"FortiOS 7.6 Administration Guide","url":"https://docs.fortinet.com/product/fortigate/7.6"}],"planWeeks":8,"hoursPerWeek":"6–8","domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Deployment and system configuration","w":24,"topics":["Initial setup: default management IP 192.168.1.99, admin account, forced password change, interface roles and access (HTTPS, SSH, ping)","Administrator accounts: admin profiles, trusted hosts, MFA for admins, password policy","Firmware management: the upgrade path, config backups and restore","VDOMs: what they separate, root VDOM, when to use multi-VDOM","FGCP HA: active-passive vs active-active, heartbeat links, primary election (monitored ports, uptime, priority, serial, override)","HA operations: session pickup, config sync, checksums, `get system ha status`, `execute ha manage`","Security Fabric: root and downstream FortiGates, authorization, FortiAnalyzer/cloud logging requirement, Security Rating","Automation stitches: triggers and actions (email, webhook, CLI script, quarantine)","Logging: log types (traffic, event, security), severity, memory/disk/FortiAnalyzer/FortiGate Cloud/syslog, log allowed traffic","FortiGate-VM and cloud deployments: VM licensing, public cloud images, cloud-native firewall concepts","Diagnostics: `get system performance status`, `diagnose sys top`, conserve mode and av-failopen, `diagnose debug flow`, `diagnose sniffer packet`"],"notes":["Exam topics: perform initial configuration, implement the Security Fabric, configure an FGCP HA cluster, configure logging, diagnose resource and connectivity problems (FortiOS 7.6)"],"labs":["Deploy the free FortiGate-VM permanent trial (FortiOS 7.2.1 or later, FortiCloud login needed) in VirtualBox, VMware Workstation Player or Proxmox. Set a static IP on port1, lock admin access with trusted hosts and save a config backup. Trial limits: 1 vCPU, 2 GB RAM, 3 interfaces, 3 firewall policies, 3 routes, low-encryption only, no FortiGuard updates or support.","On the trial VM, create a read-only admin profile for Log & Report, add an automation stitch that emails on a configuration change, then compare `get system status` with `get system performance status`.","Run `diagnose debug flow` (filter, trace start, debug enable) while pinging through the VM, and find the 'Denied by forward policy check (policy 0)' line before and after adding a policy. HA needs two licensed units, so study the FGCP election rules in the FortiOS 7.6 Administration Guide instead."]},{"id":2,"name":"Firewall policies and authentication","w":22,"topics":["Firewall policy matching: incoming/outgoing interface, source (address, user, ISDB), destination, service, schedule; top-down first match; implicit deny (policy 0)","Address objects and groups, FQDN and geography objects, Internet Service Database (ISDB) entries","Policy logging, policy lookup tool, policy ID vs sequence, schedules","Source NAT: outgoing interface address, IP pools (overload, one-to-one, fixed port range, port block allocation)","Central SNAT table and when to use it","Destination NAT with VIPs: static NAT, port forwarding, VIP groups","Firewall authentication: local users, LDAP (regular bind), RADIUS and TACACS+ servers, user groups","Active (captive portal) vs passive authentication, authentication timeouts, allowing DNS before login","FSSO: collector agent, DC agent mode vs polling mode, group filters, `diagnose debug authd fsso list`","Two-factor authentication with FortiToken"],"notes":["Exam topics: configure firewall policies, configure SNAT and DNAT options in firewall policies, configure different methods of firewall authentication, explain how to deploy and configure FSSO"],"labs":["Within the trial's 3-policy limit, build LAN-to-WAN (NAT with outgoing interface), DMZ-to-WAN and WAN-to-DMZ policies, then use the policy lookup tool and forward traffic logs to confirm which policy each test hits.","Publish a small web server (for example a Linux VM running `python3 -m http.server 8080`) through a port-forwarding VIP from external port 8443, and test it from the WAN side.","Stand up a free Windows Server evaluation domain controller or Samba AD, add it as a regular-bind LDAP server, and require an AD group on a policy with captive-portal authentication (allow DNS first)."]},{"id":3,"name":"Content inspection","w":26,"topics":["SSL/SSH inspection: certificate inspection vs deep inspection, CA trust, exemptions, certificate pinning, untrusted certificate handling","Inspection modes: flow-based vs proxy-based, set per policy; profile-based vs policy-based NGFW mode","Web filtering: FortiGuard categories and actions (allow, monitor, warning, authenticate, block), static URL filter (exempt vs allow), rating errors, overrides","DNS filtering and safe search","Application control: sensors, categories, application overrides, filter overrides, need for deep inspection","Antivirus: flow vs proxy scanning, signature databases, FortiSandbox/cloud sandbox, content disarm and reconstruction (proxy), grayware","IPS: sensors and signature filters, rate-based signatures, botnet C&C blocking, IP exemptions, fail-open","DoS policies and anomaly thresholds","Security profile logs and troubleshooting (FortiGuard connectivity, `diagnose autoupdate versions`)"],"notes":["Exam topics: inspect encrypted traffic using certificates, identify inspection modes and configure web filtering, configure application control, configure antivirus scanning modes, configure IPS"],"labs":["On the trial VM, enable deep inspection on the LAN policy, import the FortiGate CA into a test browser and compare the site certificate before and after. Note that the permanent trial only supports low encryption and gets no FortiGuard updates, so category ratings and signatures may be limited; the lab's goal is the configuration steps.","Build a web filter profile that blocks a category, then use a static URL filter to test Exempt vs Allow for one URL and watch the web filter log.","Download the EICAR test file over HTTP and HTTPS with certificate vs deep inspection and compare the antivirus log results; then add an IPS sensor filtered to server targets and read the IPS log fields."]},{"id":4,"name":"Routing","w":14,"topics":["Route lookup order: policy routes, then the routing table (longest match, distance, priority)","Static routes: administrative distance, priority, ECMP and load-balancing methods","Routing table vs routing database: `get router info routing-table all` and `database`","Reverse path forwarding (RPF) check","Link health monitors and blackhole routes","SD-WAN members and zones, and routes that point to the zone","Performance SLAs: probes, latency, jitter, packet loss, SLA targets","SD-WAN rules: manual, best quality, lowest cost (SLA), maximize bandwidth (SLA); implicit rule","SD-WAN monitoring and troubleshooting: `diagnose sys sdwan health-check`, `diagnose sys sdwan service`"],"notes":["Exam topics: configure and route packets using static routes, configure SD-WAN to load balance traffic between multiple WAN links"],"labs":["Add two default routes on the trial VM (distance 10 and 20), check `get router info routing-table all` vs `database`, then set equal distance with different priorities and check again. The trial allows only 3 static routes.","Add a link health monitor to the primary WAN gateway, block the probe target and watch the route drop from the table.","Put two WAN interfaces in an SD-WAN zone, add a ping performance SLA and a lowest cost (SLA) rule, and use `diagnose sys sdwan health-check` to see the measurements. In a home lab, a second NAT router or Linux VM can act as the second 'ISP'."]},{"id":5,"name":"VPN","w":14,"topics":["IPsec basics: IKEv1 vs IKEv2, phase 1 and phase 2, proposals, DH groups, PFS, UDP 500/4500 and NAT-T","Route-based (interface-mode) vs policy-based IPsec","Site-to-site with static peers and dial-up (dynamic) peers","Routes and firewall policies needed for tunnel traffic","Redundant VPNs: two tunnels, route distance/priority, DPD, tunnel monitoring","Topologies: hub and spoke, full mesh, partial mesh, ADVPN short-cuts","Troubleshooting: `diagnose vpn ike gateway list`, `diagnose vpn tunnel list`, `diagnose debug application ike -1`","Remote access VPN on FortiOS 7.6: FortiClient dial-up IPsec, and the SSL VPN changes in later 7.6 builds (tunnel mode removed, web mode renamed agentless VPN)"],"notes":["Exam topics: implement a meshed or partially redundant IPsec VPN (check the current exam description for remote access VPN coverage)"],"labs":["Run two FortiGate-VM trials (one per FortiCloud account) or pair one with a free strongSwan Linux VM, and build a route-based IPsec tunnel. The permanent trial only supports low-encryption ciphers, so pick proposals both sides accept.","Break phase 2 on purpose by mismatching a selector, and read the output of `diagnose debug application ike -1` until you can find the error.","Draw hub-and-spoke, full-mesh and ADVPN designs for five sites, count the tunnels and list the routes and DPD settings each needs for failover."]}],"study":{"1":[["Walk through FGCP primary election with override disabled and then enabled.","Disabled: most connected monitored interfaces, then highest HA uptime (beyond the margin), then highest priority, then highest serial. Enabled: monitored interfaces, then priority, then uptime, then serial, so the preferred unit takes back primary after it returns."],["What does session pickup do, and why is it off by default?","It syncs the session table to secondaries so TCP sessions survive a failover. It adds CPU and heartbeat load, so it is off by default and turned on when session continuity matters."],["What does a FortiGate need to become the Security Fabric root, and how does a downstream unit join?","The root needs FortiAnalyzer or cloud logging. The downstream unit enables the Fabric connection, points to the upstream IP, and the root authorizes it by serial number."],["Explain conserve mode and the av-failopen setting.","When memory crosses the red threshold, the FortiGate enters conserve mode to protect itself. av-failopen decides whether traffic needing proxy AV passes uninspected (pass), is dropped (off), or passes once then drops (one-shot)."],["List the steps to trace why a session is dropped.","diagnose debug reset, diagnose debug flow filter addr <ip>, diagnose debug flow show function-name enable, diagnose debug flow trace start <n>, diagnose debug enable. Then read the route lookup, policy match and any deny message, and finish with diagnose debug disable."]],"2":[["How does FortiGate choose a firewall policy, and what happens if none match?","It checks interfaces, source, destination, service and schedule from the top down and uses the first match. If none match, the implicit deny (policy 0) drops the traffic; its logging is off by default."],["Compare overload, one-to-one and fixed port range IP pools.","Overload shares a few public IPs among many hosts using port translation. One-to-one maps each host to one public IP with no port translation, so the pool size caps users. Fixed port range maps internal ranges to external IPs and port ranges in a predictable way, which helps logging."],["How do you publish an internal server, and what else must be in place?","Create a VIP (static NAT or port forwarding) from the external IP/port to the internal one, then an inbound policy from the WAN interface to the server's interface with the VIP as destination and only the needed services."],["Why must DNS be allowed before a captive-portal policy?","The login prompt only appears on HTTP, HTTPS, FTP or Telnet traffic, and browsers must resolve names first. A policy that allows DNS without authentication, placed above, lets the browser reach the stage where FortiGate can show the login page."],["Compare FSSO DC agent mode and polling mode.","DC agent mode installs an agent on each domain controller that pushes logon events to the collector agent in real time. Polling mode reads DC security event logs remotely from the collector or FortiGate, with no software on DCs but more delay and load."]],"3":[["What can certificate inspection do, and what does it miss?","It reads the SNI and certificate, so it can apply web categories and identify many apps. It cannot see payloads, so AV, CDR, detailed app actions and URL paths need deep inspection."],["How do you roll out deep inspection without constant certificate warnings or breaking apps?","Distribute the FortiGate CA (or an enterprise subordinate CA) to clients by GPO or MDM, exempt sensitive categories such as banking and health, and exempt pinned apps that reject re-signed certificates."],["Explain Exempt vs Allow in the static URL filter.","The static URL filter is checked first. Exempt skips all remaining web filter checks. Allow permits the URL but still sends it to later checks such as the FortiGuard category, which can still block it."],["Flow-based vs proxy-based antivirus: what are the trade-offs?","Flow scans as data passes and holds only the last packet, so it is faster with lower latency. Proxy buffers the whole file, which uses more memory but supports features such as content disarm and reconstruction and replacement messages."],["How would you tune an IPS sensor for a DMZ web server?","Filter signatures by server target, the server's OS and applications, and severity; enable botnet C&C blocking for outbound; use IP exemptions for proven false positives instead of disabling signatures; and add a DoS policy for flood thresholds."]],"4":[["How does FortiGate pick a route?","Policy routes first. Then the routing table: longest prefix match, then lowest administrative distance, then lowest priority for static routes. Equal distance and priority give ECMP."],["What is the difference between distance and priority on static routes?","A higher distance route is kept out of the active table. Routes with equal distance but different priority are both active, and the lower priority is preferred, so the backup is still usable for RPF checks."],["What does the reverse path forwarding check do?","It drops packets whose source has no route back through the interface they arrived on. It stops spoofed traffic and often explains drops after asymmetric routing changes."],["Compare the SD-WAN strategies best quality, lowest cost (SLA) and maximize bandwidth (SLA).","Best quality picks the member with the best latency, jitter, loss or bandwidth value. Lowest cost picks the cheapest member that meets the SLA. Maximize bandwidth spreads traffic across all members that meet the SLA."],["List the pieces needed for a working SD-WAN setup.","Member interfaces in an SD-WAN zone, a static route pointing to the zone, firewall policies using the zone, performance SLAs with probe servers, and SD-WAN rules for specific traffic above the implicit rule."]],"5":[["What are phase 1 and phase 2, and what does each need to match?","Phase 1 (IKE SA) authenticates peers and builds a secure channel: IKE version, mode, authentication, proposals and DH group must match. Phase 2 (IPsec SA) protects the data: proposals, PFS/DH and quick-mode selectors must match."],["Why use route-based IPsec?","It creates a tunnel interface that routes and normal policies can use, which supports backup routes, dynamic routing, SD-WAN and ADVPN. Policy-based VPNs tie the tunnel to a policy and are harder to scale."],["How do you make branch VPNs fail over between two ISPs?","Build one tunnel per ISP, add routes with a lower distance or priority on the primary tunnel, enable DPD (or a link monitor) so a dead tunnel goes down and its route is removed, and allow traffic on both tunnels in policies."],["Compare hub and spoke, full mesh and ADVPN.","Hub and spoke needs n−1 tunnels but sends spoke-to-spoke traffic through the hub. Full mesh needs n(n−1)/2 tunnels and gives direct paths. ADVPN starts as hub and spoke and builds direct spoke tunnels on demand."],["Phase 1 is up but phase 2 fails. What do you check?","Run the IKE debug and compare phase 2 proposals, PFS/DH group and local/remote selectors on both sides. Selector mismatches are the most common cause."]]},"qCount":90,"hasLessons":true,"hasLessonsEs":true,"hasPbqs":true});
